@article{sauer,
	abstract = {If T is a family of sets and A some set we denote by T ∩ A the following family of subsets of A: T ∩ A = {F ∩ A; F ϵ T}. P. Erd{\"o}s (oral communication) transmitted to me in Nice the following question: Is it true that if T is a family of subsets of some infinite set S then either there exists to each number n a set A ⊂ S with |A| = n such that |T ∩ A| = 2n or there exists some number N such that |T ∩ A| ⩽ |A|c for each A ⊂ S with |A| ⩾ N and some constant c? In this paper we will answer this question in the affirmative by determining the exact upper bound. (Theorem 2).1},
